\boxed{n + v_p(n!)}

**Lower bound.** Choose all $a_i$ congruent modulo $p$; for instance take $a_i$ so that when $k\equiv -a_1\pmod p$ the quotients $(k+a_i)/p$ run through a complete set of consecutive residues. Then $v_p(f(k))=0$ for $k\not\equiv -a_1\pmod p$, while for $k=-a_1+pt$ we have $f(k)=p^n\cdot t(t+1)\cdots(t+n-1)$ up to units. By Legendre/Kummer, $v_p(t(t+1)\cdots(t+n-1))\ge v_p(n!)$ with equality at $t=1$. Thus the attainable valuations include $0$ and then jump to at least $n+v_p(n!)$, so $m\ge n+v_p(n!)$.

**Upper bound.** Given arbitrary $f$ and $k$, let $m_0=\min_i v_p(k+a_i)$ and $S=\{i:v_p(k+a_i)=m_0\}$. Write $k+a_i=p^{m_0}w_i$ for $i\in S$ with $p\nmid w_i$. Pick $i_0\in S$ and set $k'=-a_{i_0}+p^{m_0+1}t$. Then for $i\in S$, $v_p(k'+a_i)=v_p(w_i-w_{i_0}+pt)+m_0$ (and $=m_0+v_p(pt)$ for $i=i_0$), while for $i\notin S$ the valuation stays $m_0$ for large $t$. Hence $v_p(f(k'))-v_p(f(k))$ is controlled by $\sum_{i\in S}v_p(\cdot)$, which as $t$ varies takes a positive value at most $|S|+v_p(|S|!)\le n+v_p(n!)$. Choosing $t$ to realise this gives $v_p(f(k))<v_p(f(k'))\le v_p(f(k))+n+v_p(n!)$, so $m=n+v_p(n!)$ suffices.

